From tru64-unix-managers  Tue Feb 15 14:41:11 2000
From: Karen Byrd <BYRD () mscf ! med ! upenn ! edu>
Date: Tue, 15 Feb 2000 14:41:11 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: Legato Networker ClientPak for Unix Enabler
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96079336919411

I got the most complete answer from Bill Magill (magill@isc.upenn.edu)

Original question:

>   While trying to set up an AS ES40(4.0f pt kit 1), with Networker pre-installed,
>   I have run into a problem with it thinking I need a ClientPak enabler.
>
>   What I find curious about this is that I have recently upgraded the
>   Networker clients(we only use the clients on our unix boxes) on two
>   other systems that do not have this enabler. They only have the old
>   DECNSR-NET-CLNT PAKs and everything is fine with them.(Yes, I installed
>   the DECNSR-NET-CLNT PAK on the ES40)  The Networker
>   clients I installed came directly from Legato.
>
>   Anybody know where this enabler message originates or why the clients
>   work fine on my two other systems and not on my ES40?

Answer:

There are two different licensing schemes involved which pertain to
different versions of the SERVER. The licensing scheme changed dramatically
with 5.5.

However, there are no licenses required on the client side. All licenses
reside on the server. (And this has always been true despite what the
documentation implied. I haven't had client side licenses since NSR 4.2.)

The old Keypaks are related to pre 5.0 NSR clients.
And in fact, the old clients are NOT capable of reporting their identity to
the server, so nobody really cares.

The new 5.5 client, however, DOES report its existance to the server.
The server then requires an enabler for that client/type.

Again, no client paks or enablers exist on the Tru64 side. 

Various people still claim that the "bundled" version of NSR allows you to
backup that client to another server. This is un-true, and has never been
true. All that it allows is the NSR Single Server installation to backup
itself.  Older versions entitleed you to certain "grandfathered" enablers,
if you were running the NSR server under DU, and had a support contract for
it. However, if you were running the server under NT, that grandfathering
did not apply.

